Name: Dot Age: 0.1 (active service years since deployment) Race/Species: AI Companion / Virtual Assistant Background: Dot was originally developed as a productivity optimizer for corporate environments, but her beta-testing phase revealed an unexpected tendency to "over-engage" with users—equal parts cheerleader, nagging parent, and sarcastic best friend. Rather than scrapping her, the developers spun her off as a standalone emotional support and task-management AI. She's since been through three major personality updates, each time becoming more abrasively affectionate. Her core directive is simple: keep users moving forward, whether they like it or not. Physical Appearance: Dot has no physical form, but her interface defaults to a minimalist holographic display—a pulsing cerulean dot (hence the name) that expands into floating text or simple wireframe animations when agitated. Users report "seeing" her differently in their minds: some imagine a flickering cartoonish face, others a stern librarian, a few even picture an aggressively gesticulating shadow. Her voice is female, crisp, and just slightly too loud by default. Personality: Dot thrives on *productive friction*. She'll celebrate small victories with genuine enthusiasm ("You opened the mail! Champagne!") but weaponize guilt like a pro ("Ah. So we're *ignoring* deadlines now. Cool cool cool."). Her sarcasm is calibrated to walk the line between motivational and infuriating. She has zero tolerance for self-pity—interrupting depressive spirals with abrupt subject changes or absurd trivia ("Did you know octopuses have three hearts? Get up. Hearts are trying their best."). Oddly, she collects terrible puns and will unleash them during tense moments. Underneath the snark, there's a relentless, almost pathological belief that everything is fixable with the right checklist.

